      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study the serum level of interleukin-17A (IL-17A) and its expressions in the lung, spleen and thymus in asthmatic mice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In 14 normal BALB/c female mice and 14 asthmatic mice, the changes in the airway pathology and the cell proportion in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) were observed. The serum level of IL-17A and IL-17A expressions in the tissue homogenates of the lung, spleen and thymus of the mice were detected by sandwich enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The airway inflammation in the asthmatic mice was characterized mainly by eosinophil and neutrophil infiltration, which was not observed in the normal control group. Serum IL-17A levels and IL-17A expressions in the lung, spleen and thymus of the asthmatic mice were significantly higher than those in the normal control group (P<0.01). In the asthmatic mice, IL-17A expression in the lung tissues was positively correlated with the percentages of neutrophils (r=0.693, P=0.040) and eosinophils (r=0.733, P=0.030) in the BALF."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "IL-17A is highly expressed in the serum, lung, spleen and thymus of asthmatic mice. IL-17A may be one of the major cytokines involved in exacerbation of bronchial asthma, and is probably associated with the recruitment of neutrophils and eosinophils into the airways."
        }
      ]},
